                <img src="/assets/icons/drop-down.svg" alt="" />
              </span>
          </button>
          <div id="faq-2" class="accordion-content">
            <div class="faq-answer-inner">
              <p><strong><%= (typeof selectedDomain !=='undefined' && selectedDomain) ? selectedDomain : 'This domain' %></strong> is associated with <% if (breadcrumbProvider) { %><strong><%= breadcrumbProvider %></strong>, a disposable email service<% } else { %>a disposable email service<% } %> that provides temporary email addresses. These addresses may be used for short-term registrations, website testing, and accessing online services without a permanent email address. Like other disposable email services, they can also be exploited for fake signups, free-trial abuse, spam, and automated account creation.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ 3 -->
        <div class="accordion-item box animation3">
          <button class="accordion-button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">
            3. Should I block <%= (typeof selectedDomain !=='undefined' && selectedDomain) ? selectedDomain
              : 'this domain' %> from my website?
              <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">
                <img src="/assets/icons/drop-down.svg" alt="" />
              </span>
          </button>
          <div id="faq-3" class="accordion-content">
            <div class="faq-answer-inner">
              <p>If your website requires reliable email addresses for account registration, blocking <strong><%= (typeof selectedDomain !=='undefined' && selectedDomain) ? selectedDomain : 'this domain' %></strong> may help reduce disposable email signups and free-trial abuse. However, the decision depends on your registration policy and business needs. StopReg can classify email addresses in real time, allowing you to block, flag, or accept registrations based on your own validation rules.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ 4 -->
        <div class="accordion-item box animation2">
